    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d642-a6">

                <pb facs="0761" n="701" xml:id="pg701"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Subinclosure E.]</seg></opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">W. Springer to
                            S. C. Wilson, deputy collector, Bay City,
                        Mich.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Office of the Collector of Customs</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Port Huron, Mich.</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1895-01-30"><hi rendition="#italic">January
                                30, 1895</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your letter inclosing letter
                    and other papers from B. Boutell (returned herewith), I have to say that boom
                    sticks are classified by this office as “logs and round unmanufactured timber
                    not specially enumerated or provided for in this act, paragraph 672,” and
                    consequently are admitted the same as other logs, free of duty when imported
                    into the United States. There is nothing in the tariff law which would authorize
                    us to assess duty on such importations.</p>
                <p>Respectfully,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. Springer</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Special Deputy Collector.</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
